From 5567dbce6fc8b05d1079b8c9f6b9e6fec3fdd225 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 17:53:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(header): four bugs on every page, each one a single
 declaration
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reported: the dropdowns do not open, the header runs off the right, and there
is "a double header not in the right place". Those are four separate defects
that happen to share one file. Every number below was measured in a browser
against the real CSS, not reasoned about.

1. THE MEGA-MENU WAS BEING THROWN AWAY

   .topbar-nav { overflow: clip }

   The nav box is 40px tall. The panel opens 61px below the top of it and is
   111px tall, so ONE HUNDRED PERCENT of it fell outside the clip box. The menu
   opened correctly on every click and was discarded by an ancestor. That is
   the whole of "the dropdown does not work".

2. AND IT WOULD STILL HAVE BEEN BROKEN AFTER THAT

   .mm-panel { left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%) }

   A 1120px panel centred on a trigger near the left of the bar. Measured at
   1280px: Services' left edge at -282px, Library's at -200px. Fixing only (1)
   would have shipped a menu that opens a quarter off-screen — which reads as
   broken, where "doesn't open" merely reads as absent.

   The panel is now anchored left:0/right:0 against .topbar-inner, so it is
   exactly as wide as the header shell and cannot leave the viewport at any
   width on any monitor. Verified 320→2560px. That is also what AWS does: a
   full-width sheet under the bar, not a tooltip hanging off one word.

3. THE FREE QUOTE BUTTON WAS OFF THE SCREEN

   .topbar-nav { min-width: max-content }

   which cancels the `flex: 0 1 auto` on the line above it. The comment on
   .topbar-inner says "the nav absorbs the slack"; it could not, so there was
   none. Measured: brand 191 + nav 505 + CTA 744 = 1440px of content, plus
   64px padding and 24px gaps ≈ 1496px, inside a 1280px shell. It had never
   fitted at any desktop width.

   And because .topbar was position:fixed, that overflow never reached the
   document's scrollWidth — no overflow check could see it, including the one
   this repo shipped last week. It was only visible as the primary CTA sitting
   114px past the right edge at 1474px.

   The nav can shrink now, and the bar sheds in the order a flooring company
   can most afford to lose: Send photos (124px, also on the homepage and in the
   mobile sticky bar), the Search label (80px, the magnifier and ⌘K both still
   work), the Login label (53px, the icon is the convention and the button
   keeps its aria-label). Never the phone number, the Free Quote button or the
   brand. Verified fitting at 1200, 1280, 1366, 1440, 1474, 1600, 1920, 2560.

4. THE "DOUBLE HEADER"

   .topbar { position: fixed }

   Out of flow, so it reserved no height and painted over the UtilityBar
   sitting in flow beneath it. Measured at 1474px: the strip occupied y 0–39
   and the header y 0–65. The hours and the phone number were invisible, and
   the first 26px of every page on the site was underneath the bar. Scroll far
   enough for the hide-on-scroll class to fire and the strip appeared from
   nowhere — a second bar, in the wrong place.

   Both bars now live in one <div class="site-chrome"> that is position:sticky.
   Sticky keeps them in normal flow, so they reserve their own height
   automatically — no spacer element, no padding-top constant to drift out of
   sync — and they still pin on scroll. Measured: chrome bottom 104px, main top
   104px, no overlap; after a real 207px scroll the chrome is still at top:0.
   (The scroll was asserted, because a scroll test that silently does not
   scroll passes trivially.)

   Anchors now offset by the whole chrome, not just the nav, or they land
   behind the strip — the same bug this file already fixed once for the nav.

AND ONE THAT WAS NEVER SHIPPED BUT NEARLY WAS

The first draft put the hide-on-scroll transform on .site-chrome. .mobile-sheet
is a sibling of <header>, so it is inside that wrapper, and it is
`position: fixed; inset: 0`. A transform on an ancestor becomes the containing
block for fixed descendants: the full-screen mobile drawer would have collapsed
to a 104px bar. The transform stays on .topbar, and the guard now refuses any
transform, filter or backdrop-filter on the wrapper.

scripts/verify-chrome.mjs

All five are single declarations that look harmless in review, so they get a
check rather than a memory. Five negative tests, each reintroducing one defect.

Its own first run reported five failures and every one was false: the comments
in globals.css QUOTE the broken declarations they replaced, and the parser read
prose as property values. Comments are stripped before parsing — the identical
lesson verify-destinations.mjs learned from SiteFooter's comment about the href
it used to have.

VERIFIED 320 / 390 / 430 / 768 / 1000 / 1200 / 1280 / 1366 / 1440 / 1474 / 1600
/ 1920 / 2560: no chrome-over-content overlap, utility strip visible, CTA
inside the viewport, Free Quote inside the viewport wherever it renders, both
panels fully on screen, mobile drawer still full-viewport, zero document
overflow.

47 guards green. tsc unchanged.

